;;; `std.algebra/transitive` — R;R ⊆ R, and the `(neq ?a ?c)` guard that is
;;; the only difference between it and `(compose R R R)`.
;;;
;;; ── What the rule promises ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   (transitive R)   R(a,b) ∧ R(b,c) ∧ a ≠ c ⟹ R(a,c)
;;;
;;; The module's own description is "the guarded special case of
;;; `(compose R R R)` — the guard suppresses the self-loop a bare
;;; compose-into-self would add, which keeps an irreflexive closure
;;; irreflexive". That sentence is a claim about two rules, so this file runs
;;; both, over the same two-cycle, and the answers differ by exactly the
;;; self-loops.
;;;
;;; ── What this program does to them ─────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Three relations, and the third is why there are three:
;;;
;;;   chn  (transitive chn)      A→B→C, acyclic: the rule **fires**, once, and
;;;                              derives A→C. The assertion's own test.
;;;   tr   (transitive tr)       A→B and B→A. A→B→A would close to A→A — `neq`
;;;                              refuses; B→A→B likewise. So `tr` never grows,
;;;                              and the closure of a cycle stays irreflexive.
;;;   cyc  (compose cyc cyc cyc) the same two edges with no guard: both
;;;                              self-loops appear.
;;;
;;; A two-cycle is the smallest shape on which the guard bites; a three-chain
;;; is the smallest on which the rule has anything to say at all.
;;;
;;; ── What the expectation means ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `chn` closed at three edges is what the rule *derives*. `tr` closed at two
;;; is the guard's test in the direction that finds a bug: drop the
;;; `(neq ?a ?c)` and `(tr A A)` appears as a surplus fact. `cyc` closed at
;;; four is the control — it says both rules were reached and that the
;;; difference between them is the guard rather than the fixture.
;;;
;;; `tr` and `cyc` were this file's whole subject until M1c
;;; [S1c.1.5](../../../docs/history/m1c_external_validation/README.md#s1c15--in-the-gate),
;;; and `transitive` fired **zero** times here — deliberately, because a rule
;;; tested only where it fires has had its guard tested nowhere. What changed
;;; is who the coverage gate asks: it asks this suite rather than the corpus,
;;; and `transitive`'s six other activators are all puzzles whose hierarchies
;;; are acyclic, where the guard can never be the thing that decides. `chn` is
;;; those puzzles' case, in three facts.

(import std.algebra :symbols (transitive compose))

(relation chn Node Node :why "{?1} precedes {?2}")
(relation tr  Node Node :why "{?1} reaches {?2}")
(relation cyc Node Node)

(transitive chn)
(transitive tr)
(compose    cyc cyc cyc)

(chn A B) (chn B C)
(tr  A B) (tr  B A)
(cyc A B) (cyc B A)

(query
  :goal (tr ?x ?y)
  :no-hypothesis (chn tr cyc)
  :expect (model (chn A B) (chn B C) (chn A C)
                 (tr  A B) (tr  B A)
                 (cyc A B) (cyc B A) (cyc A A) (cyc B B)))
